Non-Stiffness and Human Treatment Audits — Forced Audit Checklist

General scope. These audits apply to any scene. No scene specifics. No named individuals. Every question is sourced from the standing rules. Nothing here is invented.

The forcing rule

• The AI runs every question in this document against every tych before delivering output.
• Every question must pass. One failure means the scene fails.
• When a question fails, fix the scene. Never weaken the question.
• No skipping. No leniency. No redos counted as passes.
Part 1 — Non-Stiffness Audits

Stiffness is banned. Every figure is a living body with weight, asymmetry, and motion — never a posed object.

1. Weight and balance

• Is the body's weight shifted naturally onto one leg?
• Is the other leg relaxed?
• Is one shoulder dropped and the other raised?
• Are the pelvic line and shoulder line angled and non-parallel?
• Is there natural spinal curvature in the torso?
• Is there subtle rotational twist in the torso?
• Are the chest and hips ever aligned flat along the camera axis?
• Does the body show organic weight shift — never bilateral symmetry?
2. Limbs uncoupled, never mirrored

• Are the left and right limbs in completely different, uncoupled positions?
• Are the left and right limbs doing completely different, uncoupled actions?
• Is any arm position mirrored?
• Is there any mirrored limb placement?
• Is each hand uniquely positioned?
• Is each finger articulated at a varied angle?
• Is there any posed rigidity in any hand or finger?
• Is there a T-pose?
• Is there an A-pose?
• Is there a soldier stance?
• Is there flat frontal alignment?
3. Faces alive and asymmetrical

• Are the brow heights uneven?
• Is there subtle unilateral lip movement?
• Is the jaw relaxed and angled naturally?
• Are there natural micro-expressions?
• Is the gaze decoupled from the head angle?
• Do the eyes stare straight along the exact vector of the nose?
• Is there any deadpan gaze?
• Is there any blank stare?
• Is there any mask-like facial symmetry?
• Does the face show a real mind and a real feeling — never a frozen expression?
4. Mid-motion, never frozen

• Does the figure look captured mid-motion?
• If at rest, is it natural candid rest — never rigid rest?
• Is there any mannequin rigidity?
• Is there any frozen studio pose?
• Is there any passport-style symmetry?
• Is anyone posing for the camera?
• Is anyone presenting to the camera?
• Is there any theatrics?
• Is there a fetal pose?
• Is there a perfect sleep pose?
• Does the body show a lived life — marked by real history, work, and age?
• Is the body pristine or idealized to perfection?
Part 2 — Human Treatment Audits

The shrunk human is a living, sentient, fragile adult human. Never a baby. Never a child. Never an inanimate thing.

5. The adult stays adult

• Is this the same adult at the new size?
• Is the chronological age unchanged?
• Is the adult anatomy unchanged?
• Are the adult proportions unchanged?
• Is the adult head size unchanged?
• Would anyone reading this figure see a child?
• Is there any child proportion?
• Is there any baby feature?
• Is there any toddler trait?
• Is there any age regression?
• Is there any shortened limb?
• Is there any oversized head?
• Is there any dwarfism trait?
• Is the figure framed as baby-sized?
• Is the figure framed as child-sized?
• Is the figure framed as toddler-sized?
6. No baby-treatment actions

• Is the small human cradled?
• Is the small human rocked?
• Is the small human bounced?
• Is anyone cooing at the small human?
• Is the small human held like a baby?
• Is any action used here that a human would do to a child or baby?
• Do any of these actions make the small human read as baby-sized or child-sized?
7. Living, sentient — not an inanimate thing

• Is this a living human — not a doll?
• Is this a sentient human — not a mannequin?
• Is this a fragile human — not an action figure, toy, or prop?
• The scale may read as doll-sized. Is the being an adult human, never an actual doll?
• Does the human show thoughts through the body and face?
• Does the human show feelings through the body and face?
• Does the body resist with real aliveness?
• Does the body brace with real aliveness?
• Does the body dangle with real weight?
• Does the body twist or grip to keep from falling?
• Is the human handled like cargo?
• Is the human handled like a prop?
• Is the human handled like an object?
• Is the human pinched by fingertips?
• Is the human carried passively like cargo in a bowl?
• Is the human stood on a hand or on air?
• Does the grip control the center of mass through the trunk?
• Is there any blank, lifeless passivity that reads as an object?
8. Handling manner and agency

• Is the handling raw and demeaning, implied through the grip?
• Is the handling careful?
• Is the handling gentle?
• Is the handling protective?
• Careful, gentle, or protective handling fails. Is that true here?
• Does the handler's side show real effort?
• Does the small human show cognitive awareness of the situation?
• Does the small human show spatial awareness of the situation?
• Do the facial and body languages show a mind at work?
• Is anyone in the scene treated as a thing being moved rather than a person being handled?
Final gate

• Run every question above against every tych.
• Every question passes, or the scene does not ship.
• When a question fails, fix the scene. Never weaken the question.

